> So, something is a little fishy here.  I think there=E2=80=99s a =
chance that there is some slight brokenness with spin mutexes on Alpha =
that only show up on MULTIPROCESSOR kernels.  I=E2=80=99ll take a deeper =
look a little later today.

So, I spent a bunch of time looking at the Alpha spin lock handling code =
today, and it looks OK to me.  I don=E2=80=99t think there=E2=80=99s a =
generic problem with those here.

So let=E2=80=99s take a look at what else might be going on=E2=80=A6

pmap_tlb_shootnow() takes 2 locks:

- The tlb_lock (1st)
- The specific pmap=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cactivation lock=E2=80=9D (2nd).  =
This is a global lock hash; multiple pmaps will share a lock.

tlb_lock is a spin mutex at IPL_VM.

The pmap activation locks are spin mutexes at IPL_SCHED.

Hmm.

So, spin mutexes are kind of interesting.  For those not aware of that =
they are, in the NetBSD kernel they=E2=80=99re basically like the old =
=E2=80=9Cs =3D splbio(); =E2=80=A6 splx(s);=E2=80=9D pair, but in =
addition to blocking interrupts on the current cpu, they also block =
other CPUs from accessing data protected by that mutex.

When you acquire a spin mutex, the IPL is raised to what the mutex =
requires, and a counter is bumped (it=E2=80=99s actually decremented, =
but never mind that right now=E2=80=A6), and if the new value of the =
count is 1, then the previous IPL is stashed away in a per-cpu variable.

When a spin mutex is released, the count is dropped, and if the new =
value is 0, is restores the saved IPL.

So, if you acquire tlb_lock and then a pmap activation lock, and the =
releasing them (in either order, but in this case it=E2=80=99s the same =
order), it=E2=80=99s:

	s =3D splraise(IPL_VM) -> splraise(IPL_SCHED) -> /* nothing */ =
-> splx(s)

Now, near the top of pmap_tlb_shootnow(), we have the comment:

        /*
         * Acquire the shootdown mutex.  This will also block IPL_VM
         * interrupts and disable preemption.  It is critically =
important
         * that IPIs not be blocked in this routine.
         */

This is because while we are spinning waiting for other CPUs to process =
TLB invalidations we=E2=80=99ve signaled, we need to be able to respond =
to IPIs that they might send (they might be waiting for US to respond to =
that one before they can process the one that we=E2=80=99ve sent=E2=80=A6)=


Being at IPL_SCHED would normally block processing of IPIs sent to this =
CPU.  It=E2=80=99s kind of unfortunate that I forgot about this little =
detail of how spin mutexes work when I overhauled the Alpha pmap =
recently.

I can fix this easily enough (with a call to alpha_pal_swpipl() after =
releasing the activation lock)=E2=80=A6 but it shouldn=E2=80=99t =
actually be necessary because of another thing that the low-level spin =
lock code does=E2=80=A6

When spinning, the locking code is supposed to implement a backoff =
algorithm to reduce the expensive bus traffic that=E2=80=99s needed to =
implement the atomic memory operations underpinning the locks.  This is =
implemented by the SPINLOCK_BACKOFF() macro in <sys/lock.h>:

#define SPINLOCK_BACKOFF(count)                                 \
do {                                                            \
        int __i;                                                \
        for (__i =3D (count); __i !=3D 0; __i--) {                  \
                SPINLOCK_BACKOFF_HOOK;                          \
        }                                                       \
        if ((count) < SPINLOCK_BACKOFF_MAX)                     \
                (count) +=3D (count);                             \
} while (/* CONSTCOND */ 0);

Note that SPINLOCK_BACKOFF_HOOK =E2=80=A6 On the Alpha, this is defined =
as:

#define SPINLOCK_SPIN_HOOK                                              =
\
do {                                                                    =
\
        struct cpu_info *__ci =3D curcpu();                              =
 \
        int __s;                                                        =
\
                                                                        =
\
        if (__ci->ci_ipis !=3D 0) {                                      =
 \
                /* printf("CPU %lu has IPIs pending\n",                 =
\
                    __ci->ci_cpuid); */                                 =
\
                __s =3D splhigh();                                       =
         \
                alpha_ipi_process(__ci, NULL);                          =
\
                splx(__s);                                              =
\
        }                                                               =
\
} while (0)


I.e. while spinning, we should be able to process IPIs=E2=80=A6 but I =
wonder if this hook is not working for some reason=E2=80=A6

